WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.801
Welcome to Module 3 of the Azure Security

2
00:00:02.801 --> 00:00:06.200
Engineering learning path:
the edge and the egress.

3
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:10.812
Module 2 put a web application firewall
in front of each web workload.

4
00:00:10.812 --> 00:00:13.614
This module makes sure
nothing can go around it,

5
00:00:13.614 --> 00:00:17.207
and then turns to the other
direction: traffic leaving Azure,

6
00:00:17.207 --> 00:00:22.237
through Azure Firewall, with threat
intelligence and intrusion detection denying.

7
00:00:22.237 --> 00:00:27.317
It covers Steps 5 and 6 of the Azure
Security Engineering implementation guide,

8
00:00:27.317 --> 00:00:32.440
and takes about fifty-five minutes, plus
a lab in your own test subscription.

9
00:00:36.190 --> 00:00:37.340
Why does this matter?

10
00:00:37.340 --> 00:00:40.636
A web application firewall
in prevention mode protects

11
00:00:40.636 --> 00:00:43.652
nothing if an attacker can
call the origin directly.

12
00:00:43.652 --> 00:00:46.244
The guide's dangers
table puts it this way:

13
00:00:46.244 --> 00:00:50.969
attackers bypass the firewall and the
DDoS controls by calling the origin,

14
00:00:50.969 --> 00:00:55.847
and the Front Door backend service tag
alone admits any customer's Front Door,

15
00:00:55.847 --> 00:01:00.115
as Learn's page, Secure traffic to
Azure Front Door origins, explains.

16
00:01:00.115 --> 00:01:02.628
The other direction matters just as much.

17
00:01:02.628 --> 00:01:06.456
A workload whose traffic leaves
Azure without inspection can

18
00:01:06.456 --> 00:01:09.990
reach a command-and-control
server, or send data out, unseen.

19
00:01:09.990 --> 00:01:13.823
So this module covers both:
origins only the edge can reach,

20
00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:17.177
with TLS one point two or
later and tested failover,

21
00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:21.569
and every workload subnet's default
route through Azure Firewall,

22
00:01:21.569 --> 00:01:26.170
with threat intelligence and
intrusion detection in deny mode.

23
00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:32.833
Here's what you'll be able to
do by the end of this module.

24
00:01:32.833 --> 00:01:36.503
You'll choose Front Door
Premium, Application Gateway,

25
00:01:36.503 --> 00:01:39.032
or both, for an application, and say why.

26
00:01:39.032 --> 00:01:40.970
You'll lock an origin to the edge,

27
00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:45.220
and explain why the Front Door backend
service tag alone isn't enough.

28
00:01:45.220 --> 00:01:48.570
You'll check TLS, health
probes and failover.

29
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:52.273
You'll route a workload subnet's
default route to Azure Firewall,

30
00:01:52.273 --> 00:01:54.508
and check it with the effective routes.

31
00:01:54.508 --> 00:01:58.264
And you'll move threat intelligence
and intrusion detection from

32
00:01:58.264 --> 00:02:02.157
alert to deny through a draft, and
say what deploying a draft replaces.

33
00:02:02.157 --> 00:02:06.884
You need Module 2, and a skim of Network
and Transport, which owns the agency's

34
00:02:06.884 --> 00:02:11.880
own edge and the trusted internet
connection; the guide leaves those to it.

35
00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.855
Unit 1 is the guide's Step 5.

36
00:02:17.855 --> 00:02:20.511
Learn's overview, What
is Azure Front Door,

37
00:02:20.511 --> 00:02:25.746
says Front Door is global and sits at the
Microsoft edge with built-in layer three

38
00:02:25.746 --> 00:02:29.964
and four DDoS protection, while
Application Gateway is regional,

39
00:02:29.964 --> 00:02:32.230
in the agency's own virtual network.

40
00:02:32.230 --> 00:02:35.868
Either carries the web application
firewall from Module 2.

41
00:02:35.868 --> 00:02:38.393
The guide's selection is design guidance.

42
00:02:38.393 --> 00:02:43.115
Front Door Premium for internet-facing
applications that need global reach,

43
00:02:43.115 --> 00:02:46.067
Private Link origins or
managed firewall rules.

44
00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:50.494
Application Gateway for regional
applications, for traffic that must enter

45
00:02:50.494 --> 00:02:54.506
through the agency's own network, or
where Front Door isn't in the region.

46
00:02:54.506 --> 00:02:58.864
And both, with Front Door reaching
Application Gateway over Private Link,

47
00:02:58.864 --> 00:03:02.080
where the application needs
regional inspection too.

48
00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:07.454
One date to plan around: Learn's migration
page says classic Front Door retires on

49
00:03:07.454 --> 00:03:10.102
March thirty-first, twenty twenty-seven,

50
00:03:10.102 --> 00:03:13.600
and already accepts no
new profiles or domains.

51
00:03:17.350 --> 00:03:18.437
Now the origin.

52
00:03:18.437 --> 00:03:21.475
Use Private Link where the
origin type supports it.

53
00:03:21.475 --> 00:03:26.996
Learn's page on Private Link in Front Door
Premium lists App Service, Blob Storage,

54
00:03:26.996 --> 00:03:32.600
internal load balancers, API Management,
Application Gateway and Container Apps.

55
00:03:32.600 --> 00:03:36.249
Approve the private endpoint
connection Front Door requests,

56
00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:39.688
and don't mix public and private
origins in one origin group.

57
00:03:39.688 --> 00:03:41.757
Where Private Link doesn't apply,

58
00:03:41.757 --> 00:03:46.816
Learn's page on securing traffic to Front
Door origins says to use the Front Door

59
00:03:46.816 --> 00:03:50.419
backend service tag and the X
Azure F D I D header together,

60
00:03:50.419 --> 00:03:54.712
because the tag's addresses are
shared by every Front Door customer.

61
00:03:54.712 --> 00:03:57.788
The header carries your own
profile's Front Door I D.

62
00:03:57.788 --> 00:04:01.749
For an App Service origin, one
access restriction does both,

63
00:04:01.749 --> 00:04:04.337
as Learn's multi-region tutorial shows.

64
00:04:04.337 --> 00:04:07.445
For an application
gateway behind Front Door,

65
00:04:07.445 --> 00:04:12.870
an N S G rule allows only the tag, and a
firewall custom rule checks the header.

66
00:04:16.620 --> 00:04:18.233
Next, TLS.

67
00:04:18.233 --> 00:04:22.730
Learn's internet ingress design
guide says TLS one point two or one

68
00:04:22.730 --> 00:04:27.145
point three only, and on Application
Gateway, a predefined policy.

69
00:04:27.145 --> 00:04:30.695
The guide's command, from
Learn's Az PowerShell page,

70
00:04:30.695 --> 00:04:34.837
sets the predefined policy twenty
twenty-two oh one oh one S.

71
00:04:34.837 --> 00:04:39.739
Front Door Standard and Premium require
H T T P S on every custom domain,

72
00:04:39.739 --> 00:04:42.782
with custom certificates
kept in Key Vault.

73
00:04:42.782 --> 00:04:44.970
Then health probes and failover.

74
00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:49.793
Learn's Front Door quickstart builds an
origin group with probes and two origins in

75
00:04:49.793 --> 00:04:52.589
different regions, then
stops one origin to show

76
00:04:52.589 --> 00:04:55.595
the application stays
available through the other.

77
00:04:55.595 --> 00:04:58.045
The program exercises that twice a year.

78
00:04:58.045 --> 00:04:59.772
Cutting over is gentle too:

79
00:04:59.772 --> 00:05:04.562
run Front Door beside the old public
endpoint and compare logs before you

80
00:05:04.562 --> 00:05:09.195
change D N S, then close the origin's
public path one application at a time.

81
00:05:09.195 --> 00:05:14.130
Rollback removes the access
restriction and restores D N S.

82
00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:20.217
Unit 2 is the guide's Step 6.

83
00:05:20.217 --> 00:05:24.491
Network and Transport owns the
agency's own edge; inside Azure,

84
00:05:24.491 --> 00:05:29.518
Azure Firewall is the inspection point
for egress and east-west traffic.

85
00:05:29.518 --> 00:05:34.501
Learn's Zero Trust recommendations for
Azure Firewall check that outbound traffic

86
00:05:34.501 --> 00:05:37.135
from workloads routes
through the firewall,

87
00:05:37.135 --> 00:05:41.691
that threat intelligence and intrusion
detection are in alert-and-deny mode,

88
00:05:41.691 --> 00:05:45.393
that outbound TLS is inspected,
and that diagnostic logs are on.

89
00:05:45.393 --> 00:05:46.680
The SKU matters.

90
00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:49.905
Threat intelligence deny
needs Standard or Premium.

91
00:05:49.905 --> 00:05:54.860
Intrusion detection and prevention,
TLS inspection and full URL filtering

92
00:05:54.860 --> 00:06:00.130
need Premium, and Learn says Standard
upgrades to Premium without redeployment.

93
00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:05.193
For high-volume egress, Learn suggests
a NAT gateway on the firewall subnet,

94
00:06:05.193 --> 00:06:07.643
to avoid running out of source ports.

95
00:06:07.643 --> 00:06:13.302
And TLS inspection needs an intermediate
C A certificate in Key Vault that clients

96
00:06:13.302 --> 00:06:19.230
trust; it doesn't decrypt the Education,
Finance, Government and Health categories.

97
00:06:22.980 --> 00:06:25.270
Here are the guide's Step 6 commands.

98
00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:30.965
Create the firewall policy in alert mode,
on Premium, from Learn's C L I reference.

99
00:06:30.965 --> 00:06:36.025
Route each workload subnet's default
route, zero dot zero dot zero dot zero

100
00:06:36.025 --> 00:06:41.002
slash zero, to the firewall's private
I P, with Learn's route table commands.

101
00:06:41.002 --> 00:06:42.977
Send the logs to the workspace.

102
00:06:42.977 --> 00:06:47.432
Leave threat intelligence and intrusion
detection in alert for two weeks,

103
00:06:47.432 --> 00:06:50.454
reading their logs for hits
on legitimate traffic.

104
00:06:50.454 --> 00:06:52.704
Then move to deny through a draft,

105
00:06:52.704 --> 00:06:57.203
so every change lands at once and
can be reviewed: create the draft,

106
00:06:57.203 --> 00:07:01.613
update both modes to Deny, show it,
and deploy it in a change window.

107
00:07:01.613 --> 00:07:04.735
Learn's page, Azure Firewall
Draft plus Deployment,

108
00:07:04.735 --> 00:07:07.412
carries one warning
that the guide repeats:

109
00:07:07.412 --> 00:07:11.798
deploying replaces the whole applied
policy, so changes made to the live

110
00:07:11.798 --> 00:07:16.333
policy after the draft was created are
lost unless they're in the draft too.

111
00:07:16.333 --> 00:07:18.908
Keep one change path, drafts only.

112
00:07:18.908 --> 00:07:21.317
And east-west, as design guidance:

113
00:07:21.317 --> 00:07:26.760
spoke to spoke through the hub firewall,
with N S Gs denying by default.

114
00:07:30.510 --> 00:07:31.822
Where are agencies?

115
00:07:31.822 --> 00:07:34.985
In commercial Azure,
which a G C C tenant uses,

116
00:07:34.985 --> 00:07:38.586
both steps run as written,
but check regions first.

117
00:07:38.586 --> 00:07:41.727
Front Door Standard and
Premium are available;

118
00:07:41.727 --> 00:07:45.191
the classic tier retires on
March thirty-first, 2027,

119
00:07:45.191 --> 00:07:50.185
and Premium is needed for managed
firewall rules and Private Link origins.

120
00:07:50.185 --> 00:07:54.973
Learn limits Front Door Private Link
to regions with availability zones,

121
00:07:54.973 --> 00:07:57.485
so check the origin's region on the list.

122
00:07:57.485 --> 00:08:02.040
And Azure Firewall Premium's region
list is on the products-by-region page,

123
00:08:02.040 --> 00:08:06.147
not in Learn, so the guide records
it under what couldn't be verified.

124
00:08:06.147 --> 00:08:09.991
On other clouds, the guide
names O C I Network Firewall,

125
00:08:09.991 --> 00:08:15.057
built on Palo Alto Networks, with
intrusion detection and prevention,

126
00:08:15.057 --> 00:08:19.948
TLS inspection and URL filtering,
reached through V C N route tables.

127
00:08:19.948 --> 00:08:25.650
AWS Network Firewall and Google's
Cloud N G F W aren't compared there.

128
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:33.769
The lab runs the guide's Step 5 and
Step 6 checks in a test subscription

129
00:08:33.769 --> 00:08:38.675
where someone has applied those steps to
a test application and a pilot spoke.

130
00:08:38.675 --> 00:08:40.237
The authors haven't run it.

131
00:08:40.237 --> 00:08:42.425
One part writes, and it's labeled.

132
00:08:42.425 --> 00:08:45.555
Step one calls the origin's
own host name directly,

133
00:08:45.555 --> 00:08:48.150
then the same request through Front Door.

134
00:08:48.150 --> 00:08:52.164
The guide's Validate step says
the direct request is refused,

135
00:08:52.164 --> 00:08:56.500
a four oh three or no route, and
the one through Front Door succeeds.

136
00:08:56.500 --> 00:08:59.664
Step two exports the
origin's access restriction:

137
00:08:59.664 --> 00:09:02.750
look for the service tag
and the header together.

138
00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.612
Step three checks the
application gateway's TLS policy,

139
00:09:06.612 --> 00:09:08.963
the predefined one the guide sets.

140
00:09:08.963 --> 00:09:13.970
Step four is the failover test, and it
writes, so use only the test application:

141
00:09:13.970 --> 00:09:17.150
stop the second origin
and call Front Door again.

142
00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.475
The application should stay
available through the other origin.

143
00:09:20.475 --> 00:09:23.410
Step five starts the stopped origin again.

144
00:09:27.160 --> 00:09:30.698
Step six reads the test
V M's effective routes.

145
00:09:30.698 --> 00:09:33.702
The guide's Validate step
says the default route,

146
00:09:33.702 --> 00:09:38.284
zero dot zero dot zero dot zero slash
zero, goes to the firewall's private I P.

147
00:09:38.284 --> 00:09:42.265
Step seven checks the firewall
policy, with the guide's own query:

148
00:09:42.265 --> 00:09:46.547
threat intelligence and intrusion
detection should both read Deny.

149
00:09:46.547 --> 00:09:50.092
Step eight runs on the test V
M: request a test domain your

150
00:09:50.092 --> 00:09:54.098
security monitor confirms is on
the threat intelligence feed.

151
00:09:54.098 --> 00:09:55.958
The request should be denied;

152
00:09:55.958 --> 00:09:59.523
curl prints three zeros when
the connection is refused.

153
00:09:59.523 --> 00:10:04.185
Step nine, back on your machine,
finds the denied request in the log.

154
00:10:04.185 --> 00:10:08.241
The guide sends the firewall's
logs to resource-specific tables,

155
00:10:08.241 --> 00:10:11.761
and Learn's Azure Firewall
monitoring reference names

156
00:10:11.761 --> 00:10:14.898
the threat intelligence
table A Z F W Threat Intel.

157
00:10:14.898 --> 00:10:18.522
Step ten backs it out: step five
already started the origin,

158
00:10:18.522 --> 00:10:22.598
so confirm it reads Running and
answers through Front Door again.

159
00:10:22.598 --> 00:10:24.310
Nothing else changed.

160
00:10:28.060 --> 00:10:33.115
The federal requirement, from NIST special
publication eight hundred fifty-three,

161
00:10:33.115 --> 00:10:36.485
revision five, as the guide's
requirements table maps it.

162
00:10:36.485 --> 00:10:40.694
Step 5 supports S C seven,
boundary protection, S C eight,

163
00:10:40.694 --> 00:10:45.089
transmission confidentiality
and integrity, and C P two,

164
00:10:45.089 --> 00:10:49.485
the contingency plan, which
the failover test exercises.

165
00:10:49.485 --> 00:10:55.185
Its evidence is the origin lock-down test,
the TLS policy and the failover test.

166
00:10:55.185 --> 00:11:00.815
Step 6 supports S C seven and its enhancement
five, deny by default, and S I four,

167
00:11:00.815 --> 00:11:04.925
system monitoring; its evidence
is the firewall policy,

168
00:11:04.925 --> 00:11:07.785
the routes and the denied test request.

169
00:11:07.785 --> 00:11:10.520
Each row lines up with the fed-ramp twenty

170
00:11:10.520 --> 00:11:13.335
X key security indicators the guide names.

171
00:11:13.335 --> 00:11:18.277
The steps support these controls; the
organization still sets their parameters,

172
00:11:18.277 --> 00:11:21.810
and the assessor decides
whether each is satisfied.

173
00:11:25.560 --> 00:11:27.535
Check yourself with two questions.

174
00:11:27.535 --> 00:11:30.309
First: an App Service
origin allows traffic

175
00:11:30.309 --> 00:11:33.160
only from the Front Door
backend service tag.

176
00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:34.797
Is it locked to your Front Door?

177
00:11:34.797 --> 00:11:36.335
The answer is b, no.

178
00:11:36.335 --> 00:11:40.544
The tag's addresses are shared
by every Front Door customer,

179
00:11:40.544 --> 00:11:44.410
so check the X Azure F D I D
header too, or use Private Link.

180
00:11:44.410 --> 00:11:49.210
Second: someone changed the live firewall
policy after a draft was created.

181
00:11:49.210 --> 00:11:51.385
What happens when the draft is deployed?

182
00:11:51.385 --> 00:11:55.238
The answer is b: the draft
replaces the whole applied policy,

183
00:11:55.238 --> 00:11:58.697
and the live change is lost
unless it's in the draft too.

184
00:11:58.697 --> 00:12:03.970
The module page has three more questions,
each linked to the unit that answers it.

185
00:12:07.720 --> 00:12:08.545
To sum up.

186
00:12:08.545 --> 00:12:12.369
You chose the edge for an
application and locked its origin,

187
00:12:12.369 --> 00:12:14.320
so a direct request is refused.

188
00:12:14.320 --> 00:12:17.258
You checked TLS, and tested failover.

189
00:12:17.258 --> 00:12:21.334
You checked that the pilot subnet's
egress routes to Azure Firewall,

190
00:12:21.334 --> 00:12:25.191
with threat intelligence and
intrusion detection in deny mode,

191
00:12:25.191 --> 00:12:27.520
and found the denied request in its log.

192
00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.454
The evidence an I S S O keeps from this
module: the profile, origin group,

193
00:12:32.454 --> 00:12:36.263
route and access-restriction
exports; the TLS policy;

194
00:12:36.263 --> 00:12:39.812
the refused direct request
and the failover test;

195
00:12:39.812 --> 00:12:43.014
the firewall policy
export and route tables;

196
00:12:43.014 --> 00:12:48.208
the denied request and its log row;
and the bypass and allow-list register.

197
00:12:48.208 --> 00:12:51.701
Next is Module 4: Private
Link, D N S and Key Vault,

198
00:12:51.701 --> 00:12:57.370
taking pass off the internet and
putting vaults behind role-based access.
